P3. Exit status and signals.

proot runs as two processes. The waiter is the process the caller started: it
traces nothing and exits with vpid 1's wait status, which the tracer writes
into a close-on-exec pipe when it reaps vpid 1, re-raising a signal with the
default action. When the pipe closes with no status, the waiter blocks every
signal, reaps the tracer and exits with its status. The tracer is its child,
dies with it through PR_SET_PDEATHSIG until vpid 1 is reaped, and sets
PTRACE_O_EXITKILL, so the tracees die with the tracer. vpid 1 dies with the
tracer until that option covers it.

A signal another process sends to the waiter goes to the tracer, and one the
waiter sends to the tracer goes to vpid 1. The tracer drops other senders'
signals and the kernel's, the terminal's included. A fault of either process
(SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGILL, SIGFPE, SIGTRAP, SIGSYS, SIGABRT from the kernel)
kills it, and the tracer's kills every tracee first. Job-control signals keep
their default action in both. Once vpid 1 is reaped, the tracer stops
forwarding, clears its death signal, points 2 at /dev/null and calls setsid();
only then are SIGHUP and SIGPIPE ignored and any other signal but the
waiter's given its default action.

The tracer points 0 and 1 at /dev/null and closes the other descriptors it
inherited, but 2, once vpid 1 is forked. The --sysvipc helper is exec'd
through proot_path and points 2 at /dev/null. The ptrace emulation kills the
ptracees of a ptracer that set PTRACE_O_EXITKILL when that ptracer exits.

